557	\subsection{Summary of Key Findings}
558	\label{subsec:pahd_motivation_summary}
559	
560	The cross-pathogen benchmark supports pathogen-adaptive method selection through two primary analyses---the scoring~$\times$~pathogen $\omega^2$ interaction, and a single-anchor vaccine-escape enrichment audit (HIV-1 as primary external anchor, with H3N2 self-consistency and SARS-CoV-2 exploratory checks)---plus two null-consistent corroborations (LOPO 0/11 and Friedman). The four lenses re-parameterize the same dataset and are not independent replications.
561	
562	\begin{enumerate}
563	\item \textbf{Interaction dominance (modeled)}: Among the modeled factors, the scoring $\times$ pathogen interaction ($\omega^2 = 0.296$; 95\% cluster-bootstrap CI [0.195, 0.333]) is the largest component, exceeding all main effects and other two-way interactions; a within-cell residual $\omega^2 \approx 0.39$ captures the unmodeled three-way interaction and parameter-variant noise. The optimal information source is pathogen-dependent.
564	\item \textbf{Unique best combinations}: 11 unique best combinations were observed across 11/11 pathogens, with 9 distinct scoring types. No two pathogens share the same best combination.
565	\item \textbf{Generalization failure}: LOPO cross-validation yielded 0/11 combination matches with a mean generalization MCC of 0.032. The best combination selected from training pathogens does not generalize to new pathogens.
566	\item \textbf{No universally superior combination}: Friedman $\chi^2 = 7.69$, $p = 0.990$. No single combination is reliably distinguishable as superior across all pathogens.
567	\end{enumerate}
568	
569	Together, the four lenses provide consistent evidence that the best-performing method differs by pathogen and that the scoring~$\times$~pathogen interaction is the largest modeled variance component. The next section investigates \textit{why} this interaction arises by analysing individual information types.
570	\phantomsection\label{subsec:pahd_prototype} % retained for cross-reference compatibility
575	Six of the 11 pathogens (SARS-CoV-2, H3N2, HIV-1, RSV, Rabies, EV-A71) have DMS-based Layer~C ground truth while five (Dengue, Norovirus, Influenza~B, MERS, HCV) have only Layer~A. A legitimate concern is whether the benchmark systematically under-performs on DMS-unavailable pathogens---if so, the main findings could reflect ground-truth quality rather than biological signal.
576	
577	Table~\ref{tab:dms_subgroup} reports per-pathogen best MCC (across the 780 scoring--detection combinations) and subgroup means. The DMS-available subgroup's best-per-pathogen MCC averages 0.287 (range 0.196--0.534); the DMS-unavailable subgroup averages 0.407 (range 0.196--0.660). A Mann--Whitney $U$ test on these two best-MCC sets yields $U = 10$, $p = 0.43$ (two-sided), indicating no statistically significant difference in achievable performance between subgroups at the given sample sizes.
