613	\section{Information-Type Analysis}
614	\label{sec:information_analysis}
615	\label{ch:adapt} % retained for cross-reference compatibility (formerly Chapter 5)
616	
617	The 20 scoring formulas used in the benchmark are derived from 10 underlying biological features spanning 6 information categories. Several scoring formulas represent the same feature (e.g., freq, freq\_with\_indel, and rare\_freq are all frequency-based), while composite formulas (EVEscape\_composite, EqualWeight) combine multiple features. The following analysis examines the discriminative power of the 10 underlying features to explain why the scoring $\times$ pathogen interaction dominates.
618	
619	The preceding sections demonstrated that the best-performing scoring--detection combination differs across pathogens ($\omega^2_{\text{scoring}\times\text{pathogen}} = 0.296$). This section investigates \textit{why} this interaction arises by analyzing the discriminative power of individual information types (features) that underlie the scoring formulas, and examines whether pathogen-specific feature importance patterns explain the observed cross-pathogen heterogeneity.
620	
621	%% ------------------------------------------------------------
622	\subsection{Per-Feature Discriminative Power}
623	\label{subsec:per_feature_auc}
624	
625	Ten positional features were evaluated as individual predictors of Layer~A (convergent evolution) ground truth across all 11 benchmark pathogens. These features represent six distinct information categories:
626	
627	\begin{itemize}
628	\item \textbf{Frequency-based} (3 features): \textit{freq} (proportion of sequences carrying a non-reference amino acid at each position), \textit{entropy} (Shannon entropy measuring allelic diversity), and \textit{rare\_freq} (frequency of minor alleles below 5\% threshold, capturing low-frequency variants that may signal emerging adaptive mutations).
629	\item \textbf{Phylogeny-based} (1 feature): \textit{homoplasy} (number of independent mutation origins inferred by Fitch parsimony on the phylogenetic tree, distinguishing convergent evolution from founder-effect-driven frequency).
630	\item \textbf{Evolutionary rate} (1 feature): \textit{dN/dS proxy} (nonsynonymous substitution rate normalized by codon degeneracy of the consensus amino acid; since only protein MSAs were available, this approximates true dN/dS without nucleotide codon alignment).
631	\item \textbf{Deep learning-based} (2 features): \textit{esm2\_llr} (ESM-2 protein language model log-likelihood ratio~\cite{lin2023esm2,rives2021biological}, measuring how surprising a mutation is relative to learned evolutionary constraints from 250M protein sequences) and \textit{semantic change} (mean cosine distance between wild-type and mutant ESM-2 embeddings at each position~\cite{hie2021learning}, measuring how much a mutation alters the protein's functional context).
632	\item \textbf{Structure-based} (2 features): \textit{plddt} (AlphaFold2/ESMFold predicted local distance difference test score~\cite{jumper2021alphafold}, indicating structural confidence and disorder propensity) and \textit{SASA} (relative solvent accessible surface area computed from ESMFold-predicted structures using the Shrake--Rupley algorithm~\cite{shrake1973environment}, measuring surface exposure).
633	\item \textbf{Chemical property} (1 feature): \textit{Grantham distance} (mean physicochemical distance between the consensus and observed variant amino acids at each position~\cite{grantham1974amino}, capturing whether substitutions are conservative or radical).
634	\end{itemize}
635	
636	These 10 features were selected from a broader set of 22 candidate information types identified from the literature. Features excluded from the analysis include: weighted contact number (WCN, high correlation with SASA, $r > 0.7$), intrinsic disorder prediction (largely redundant with pLDDT $< 50$~\cite{wilson2022alphafold}), co-mutation/mutual information (pairwise rather than per-position feature, incompatible with the univariate per-position analysis framework; additionally $O(L^2)$ computational cost), codon usage bias (weak association with hotspot status), and epitope/glycosylation distance (not uniformly available across all 11 pathogens). The selection criteria were: (1)~independence from existing features, (2)~applicability to all 11 pathogens, and (3)~prior validation in the literature.
